pergunta:
What is the best design pattern to use if I have two entities and each entity has repositories. I have 
@Entity
@Table(name = "doctor_account")
public class DoctorAccount {

    @Id
    private Integer id;

    private Integer specialtyId;

    @Column(unique=true)
    private String phone;

    private String passwordHash;

    private String fullName;

@Entity
@Table(name = "user_account")
public class UserAccount {
    @Id
    Private Integer id;

    @Column(unique=true)
    private String phone;

    private String passwordHash;

    private String fullName;

I have a service layer that takes id as input to get an entity. Id that is in user_account is not in doctor_account and vice versa. Imagine in future new user types will be added, then we need to use design patern for future code changes. What is the best design pattern to use? Give an example of the code. I am using spring framework java, spring data.

resposta:
You're encountering a classic **N-Gnerous (plural) Repository** pattern or **Abstract Repositories** challenge, especially when you want to scale your code to future new entities without modifying existing services. Since each entity has its own repository and the IDs (`id` of `UserAccount` ≠ `id` of `DoctorAccount`), you should consider using **Repository Interfaces** with **CrudRepository** or **PagingAndSortingRepository**, and possibly **custom repository methods** if needed.

But in your case, you don't have any shared logic at the repository level — each entity is independent. So the **best pattern** is to **keep them separate**, but use **interfaces** to avoid code duplication and improve maintainability. Over time, if you add new entities, you can simply create new repositories without touching existing ones — that's already the most scalable design.

However, if you're looking to **refactor or consolidate** logic (e.g., common validation, conversion, or form handling), then you can consider using **Adapter Pattern**, **Decorator Pattern**, or **Facade Pattern** — but at **repository level**, the cleanest solution is to use **repositories as components**.

---

### ✅ Recommended Design Pattern: **Repository Interfaces (Standard CRUD Pattern)**

#### 👉 No need for complex patterns yet — use standard `CrudRepository` or create your own!

Since you're using Spring Data JPA, you can use `CrudRepository` to avoid boilerplate.

#### ✅ Example: Using Spring Data JPA

Update your services to use `CrudRepository`:

```java
@SpringBootApplication
public class AppConfig {
    // You don't need an additional @Configuration here since @RestController and @Service auto-configures
}
```

Your services now look like:

```java
@Service
public class DoctorService {

    private final DoctorRepository doctorRepository;

    public DoctorService(DoctorRepository doctorRepository) {
        this.doctorRepository = doctorRepository;
    }

    public DoctorAccount findById(Long id) {
        return doctorRepository.findById(id).orElse(null);
    }

    public List<DoctorAccount> findAll() {
        return doctorRepository.findAll();
    }
}
```

And the service layer configures the repository:

```java
@Configuration
public class RepositoryConfig {

    @Bean
    public DoctorRepository doctorRepository(DoctorEntityType entityType) {
        return new DoctorCrudRepository<DoctorAccount>(entityType);
    }
}
```

And define the `CrudRepository` interface:

```java
@Repository
public interface DoctorCrudRepository<T> extends org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.CrudRepository<T, Integer>, org.springframework.data.publication.WeakReferenceSecurityDeleted<T> {}

@Entity
public class DoctorEntityType {
    private static final String ENTITY_TYPE = "doctor_account";
}
```

Similarly for `UserAccount`:

```java
@Entity
public class UserEntityType {
    private static final String ENTITY_TYPE = "user_account";
}
```

```java
public interface UserCrudRepository<T> extends org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.CrudRepository<T, Integer>, org.springframework.data.publication.WeakReferenceSecurityDeleted<T> {}
```

And configure in `DoctorsConfig`:

```java
@Configuration
public class DoctorsConfig {
    @Bean
    public DoctorRepository doctorRepository(DoctorRepositoryFactory bean) {
        return new DoctorCrudRepository<>(bean);
    }

    @Bean
    public UserRepository userRepository(UserRepositoryFactory bean) {
        return new UserCrudRepository<>(bean);
    }
}
```

```java
@Configuration
public class UserConfig {
    @Bean
    public UserRepository userRepository(UserRepositoryFactory bean) {
        return new UserCrudRepository<>(bean);
    }

    @Bean
    public DoctorRepository doctorRepository(DoctorRepositoryFactory bean) {
        return new DoctorCrudRepository<>(bean);
    }
}
```

> Note: The `DoctorRepositoryFactory` and `UserRepositoryFactory` would be small interfaces that just wrap the concrete entity types.

---

### 🔁 Future Scalability

When adding a new entity (e.g., `PatientAccount`), you do this:

```java
@Entity
public class PatientEntityType {
    private static final String ENTITY_TYPE = "patient_account";
}
```

```java
@Configuration
public class PatientsConfig {
    @Bean
    public PatientRepository patientRepository(PatientRepositoryFactory bean) {
        return new PatientCrudRepository<>(bean);
    }
}
```

No changes needed to existing services or repositories.

---

### 🚀 When to Use More Advanced Patterns?

If your repository logic becomes complex (e.g. complex validation, conversion, domain logic), then consider:

#### ✅ **Adapter Pattern (for integrating different data sources / formats)**

```java
public interface DashboardRepositoryAdapter {
    List<DashboardUserInfo> getDtos(DoctorAccount doctor);
    List<DashboardDashboardInfo> getDtos(UserAccount user);
}
```

But at this stage, **overhead**. Stick to clean interfaces.

---

### ✅ Summary

| Goal | Pattern |
|------|--------|
| Scale to new entities | **Repository Interface + @EntityType** |
| Avoid code duplication | **Interceptors (binder, service)** |
| Maintain testability | **Stub-based testing** |
| Future logic | Add new interfaces, not modify existing ones |

---

### ✅ Recommended Architecture

```
┌───────────────────┐     ┌─────────────────────┐     ┌──────────────────────┐
│ DoctorRepository  │     │   UserRepository    │     │ DashboardModel      │
├───────────────────┤     └────────────────────→───┤     (common mapping)│
│ (Spring Crud)     │     │   (Spring Crud)    │     ┌──────────────────────┤
└───────────────→────┬───────────────────→─────────┘     └──────────────────────┤
                      ▼
                   ┌──────────────────────┐
                   │                 ▼
         ┌─────────────┐         ┌──────────────────────────────────┐
         │  DoctorService │     │  UserAccountService               │
         └─────────────┘         └──────────────────────────────────┘
                      ▼                         ▼
         ┌──────────────────────┐     ┌──────────────────────┐
         │   Sync & Business   │     │   Admin Service       │
         └──────────────────────┘     └──────────────────────┘
               → UI / API / Batch → 
                                         → User Interface
```

---

### ✅ Final Thoughts

> **You're already using the right degree of abstraction. Just use `@DataJpaRepository` or @Repository with a concrete interface. No complex pattern is needed now — just interceptor‑based service layer and future repository interfaces for scalability.**

Let me know if you want the full working sample with `@DataJpaRepository` and these interfaces — I can generate that too!

